## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: a9c1ded76ed1fc157add8ef73565bd6112967bf1

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I traced the full history of `ArenaListener.java`'s `onFoodLevelChange` method with `git log --follow -p`, since the fix commit modifies that exact handler. Searching for prior changes touching `lockFoodLevel` inside this method quickly surfaced the commit that restructured the method's logic.

**(2) The introducing change:** Commit `a9c1ded76ed1fc157add8ef73565bd6112967bf1` ("Food levels no longer deplete for players in the lobby and spectator area", 2019-07-20) refactored `onFoodLevelChange` from:

```java
if (!arena.isRunning()) return;
if (!(event.getEntity() instanceof Player) || !arena.inArena((Player) event.getEntity())) return;
if (lockFoodLevel) event.setCancelled(true);
```

to:

```java
if (!(event.getEntity() instanceof Player)) return;
Player p = (Player) event.getEntity();
if (arena.isRunning()) {
    if (lockFoodLevel) {
        event.setCancelled(true);
    }
} else {
    if (arena.inLobby(p) || arena.inSpec(p)) {
        event.setCancelled(true);
    }
}
```

While adding lobby/spectator handling for the "not running" branch, it dropped the `arena.inArena(p)` guard from the `isRunning()` branch. This meant that whenever an arena was running, *every* player's food-level events were cancelled if `lockFoodLevel` was true — even players who were not actually in that arena — instead of only those inside it.

**(3) Fix confirmation:** The bug-fixing commit `5058c47` restores exactly this missing guard by changing `if (lockFoodLevel)` back to `if (arena.inArena(p) && lockFoodLevel)` in the same branch, which is the precise piece of logic removed by `a9c1ded76`. `git merge-base --is-ancestor` confirms `a9c1ded76` is an ancestor of the fix commit, so it is reachable in HEAD's history.

I'm confident in this identification — the removed condition and the re-added condition are textually identical (`arena.inArena((Player) event.getEntity())` / `arena.inArena(p)`), making this a very strong match rather than a coincidental correlation.

